{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1390069-mages-aren-t-stupid-for-the-same-reason-buddhists-aren-t-re-gods-in-disguise","id":1390069,"title":"Mages aren't stupid for the same reason Buddhists aren't. re: \"Gods in Disguise.\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-19T14:45:59-07:00","id":1390069,"title":"Mages aren't stupid for the same reason Buddhists aren't. re: \"Gods in Disguise.\"","content":"It got brought up in the recent long thread about the Traditions, and it reminded me of an apparent disconnect that I see around this topic. The Purple Paradigm, AKA &quot;We are Gods in Disguise,&quot; gets talked about like it's an actual secret that Mages suddenly understand when they reach a certain Arete, and that it negates whatever paradigm they had before. It would be a hard secret to contain, first of all. Something like it may come to your mind spontaneously if you encountered other mages with other paradigms, as Tradition mages tend to do. The thing is, knowing it is true does not mean the mage has fully internalized it. It is an abstract meta-paradigm that doesn't suggest any sort of path from an acolyte's base existence. Not without some other framework of belief.<br /> <br /> Some real-life Buddhist traditions teach that a person on some level everyone is already enlightened, and &quot;attaining enlightenment&quot; is just the mundane aspect of the self accepting that fact. This is widely-known enough that if you live in a culture where Buddhism predominates you have probably heard it. So why aren't all the tens or hundreds of millions of Buddhist who's heard that and believe it instantly enlightened? It's that mundane aspect of the self getting in the way. It's a truckload of biological impulses and cognitive habits that thwart acceptance of that idea on a deep enough level. So maybe you become a monk. At this point you almost certainly know that your higher self is enlightened, and because you're a monk so you are motivated to become enlightened. So now you're enlightened, right? No, first you dedicate years to meditation, ritualized tasks, chanting, various kinds of abstinence, contemplation of abstract patterns, contemplation of Bodhisattvas and other intermediaries, and so on. At a very abstract level these things don't matter in themselves--how could they, if on some level really already enlightened? But they are still necessary because they are a bridge between the heavily ingrained day-to-day way of thinking and the transcendent truth. Maybe not the only bridge. Some Buddhist traditions accept the idea of a left-hand path where you can indulge in the things you'd normally avoid, to ultimately experience their emptiness. There is no contradiction here.<br /> <br /> A lot of Christians believe that it is faith in the redeeming blood of Christ, not one's worldly works, that brings you to the kingdom of Heaven. Yet faithful Christians are still likely to do good works. Even saints and prophets did.* It makes it easier to internalize the transcendent truth if you do stuff like help others and pray and so on. At a very abstract level the specifics don't matter--how could they, if on some level you're already forgiven? But maybe you find religion-based analogies alienating. Self-esteem then. People with healthy self-esteem are generally good at internal validation. They've internalized self-love enough that if someone doesn't like them they can shrug it off. They don't rely on a constant stream of praise like narcissists do. But almost all of these people still try to be likable, to achieve things that will get them prestige and avoid things that will get them scorn. It's backup. Internal validation only goes so far; if the whole world tells you that you suck it will probably get to you, whatever pure and beautiful spark of consciousness you believe you have inside. The baseline human tendency will be there. The internal validation becomes less strained if the person can build external sources of validation to draw on.<br /> <br /> Hopefully I've illustrated how the Traditions could espouse (correctly by the metaphysics of the setting) that a human being is a god in disguise, but nonetheless individuals and individual Traditions would have their own paradigms alongside that notion. A human may be god in disguise, but it takes a lot of work to peel that disguise back.<br /> <br /> *Like with an Ascended mage, at that point the practice becomes what they do <i>because</i> they've ascended, an <i>expression </i>of ascension. Paradigms don't go away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T04:35:49-07:00","id":1390528,"title":"","content":"Yeah, this is the norm for the Mahayana tradition, which accounts for the vast majority of Buddhism worldwide. Similar to the idea that Nirvana and Samsara are the same reality viewed from different perspectives, a flawed person is a manifestation of perfect Buddha Nature.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s what the Zen sages mean when they say there\u2019s nothing to achieve: it doesn\u2019t mean you don\u2019t practice, but that Awakening isn\u2019t the result of practice. It\u2019s not something you can bring about by doing a thing, because then it would be a conditioned phenomenon and, being subject to conditionality, also be impermanent. In other words, then you\u2019d stop being enlightened Once the conditions for your enlightenment inevitably ceased to be present. <br /> <br /> Instead the idea is that Awakening represents our true nature to begin with, which is why sudden enlightenment is possible. It also leads to the idea of why practice is important: if Awakened Mind is your true nature, not a switch you flip on at some point, then how better to manifest that nature than through practice?<br /> <br /> Similarly, if you see mages\u2019 true nature as existing outside of time and phenomenal reality, then it makes sense for them to try to manifest some approximation of it <i>within </i>phenomenal reality through their practices, to be more \u201cthemselves,\u201d as it were.<br /> <br /> Good topic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T07:46:18-07:00","id":1390551,"title":"","content":"Paradigms are very ample, &quot;We are God(s) in Disguise&quot; only corresponds with the Purple Paradigm under some interpretations (and it's not the only Paradigm that can correspond to the Purple Paradigm - Chaos Magick can wear Pruple too, and likely others can with some tweaks).<br /> <br /> I.E: Verbena may internalize this as having the Blood of the Wyck running trough their veins. This would make *you* a God in Disguise, but doesn't preclude the existence of *other* gods or their importance to magic(k). One could even argue that not everyone has that blood, and you were lucky. That wouldn't correspond perfectly with Mage rules, but it's a valid interpretation of what being &quot;a god in disguise&quot; can mean for a given Tradition<br /> <br /> Important detail: &quot;We are God(s) in Disguise&quot; it's a Paradigm related with &quot;the Traditions&quot;, yes? - Well, them being objectively and obviously righ<i>t would make mages as a whole very stupid</i>: there's a lot of mages outside the Traditions (are all those mages stupid?).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-21-2020, 10:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T09:22:49-07:00","id":1390561,"title":"","content":"It's not just \u201cWe're All Gods in Disguise\u201d; the Purple Paradigm itself is something that you don't have to be stupid to not accept. For one thing, it was devised during the founding of the Traditions \u2014 which means that it wasn't around before then. This right here undermines any argument that mages know in their guts that Magick is fundamentally the same for everyone, that ultimately it's all just Willworking: if they knew it in their guts, it would have been a known fact for as long as there have been mages. <br /> <br /> I don't know if there are any mage factions that officially don't accept that it's all just Willworking (other than the Technocracy, which clearly and unambiguously draws a line between \u201cwhat <i>they</i> do\u201d and \u201cwhat <i>we</i> do\u201d). Certainly, the Ahl-i-Batin, Children of Knowledge, and Hollow Ones clearly accept the Purple Paradigm (or at least did in the past). Whether or not any of the other Disparates do is an open question; but I do think that it's conceivable that many or even most of them don't. They don't vehemently reject it the way the Union does; but I can definitely see the Wu Lung, for instance, looking at the notion that everyone's doing basically the same thing as them and going \u201cmeh\u2026\u201d<br /> <br /> Faradn has a valid point in that \u201cWe're All Gods in Disguise\u201d and other expressions of the Purple Paradigm don't displace other paradigms, and you don't have to treat other beliefs about how magick works as window dressing in order to accept the idea that it's all fundamentally the same thing. But it's not something that comes naturally, and has to be taught; and my own view is that it generally <i>isn't</i> taught outside the Traditions. <br /> <br /> The Traditions aren't objectively and obviously right about the Purple Paradigm, in large part because of Practices. Witchcraft looks nothing like Weird Science, and its profoundly bizarre that a Verbena can look at what an Etherite does (or vice versa) and say \u201cthat's fundamentally the same as what I do\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-22-2020, 12:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T10:13:15-07:00","id":1390569,"title":"","content":"The Techgnosi/Scientific Mystic merit lets a character say, I don't get that right now, but it's all from Will channeled through instruments.<br /> <br /> The Purple Paradigm is a game conceit to make it so Traditions mages can cast together. If we're looking at more personal and close connections, it should take a lot of effort for the witch and weird scientist to agree on how to cast together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T12:32:37-07:00","id":1390601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1390069-mages-aren-t-stupid-for-the-same-reason-buddhists-aren-t-re-gods-in-disguise?p=1390569#post1390569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Techgnosi/Scientific Mystic merit lets a character say, I don't get that right now, but it's all from Will channeled through instruments.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a reason for that slash in the middle of the name: Scientific Mystic lets a technomancer engage in traditionally mystical Practices from a scientific perspective; and Techgnosi lets a mystic engage in traditionally technomantic Practices from a mystical perspective. They're two different and mutually opposed things. Techgnosi is arguably as you say; Scientific Mystic arguably is not. <br /> <br /> Incidentally, this pair of merits only makes sense as merits if you actually get some benefit out of them. For that reason, I generally don't use them in my games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1390069-mages-aren-t-stupid-for-the-same-reason-buddhists-aren-t-re-gods-in-disguise?p=1390569#post1390569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Purple Paradigm is a game conceit to make it so Traditions mages can cast together. If we're looking at more personal and close connections, it should take a lot of effort for the witch and weird scientist to agree on how to cast together.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that is arguably the main advantage that the Traditions have over the Disparates. for all the talk about how much they squabble amongst themselves, the Traditions have come to a mutual understanding as to the nature of Magick, and understanding that allows a Verbena and an Etherite to smooth over the rough edges in trying to combine their differing Practices. That's not to say that, say, an Orphan technomancer and a Sister of Hypollyta can't combine their Practices; just that they generally <i>won't even try</i>: to the extent they work together, it's more likely to be each of them doing their own thing in a way that complements what the other is doing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-21T21:39:23-07:00","id":1390701,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a>, yeah I guess I was mainly thinking about Tradition mages. I imagine a Tradition acolyte could be taught the basic WAGID idea pretty early, though it would depend on the specific mentor or chantry and how involved they are in larger Council culture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}